\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{The unselected graph does not determine the retained law}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On selected backgrounds, the retained-precision spectrum does
not factor through the isomorphism class of the unselected weighted
incidence data #{(\Pi,w,m)}. In particular, that unselected class
does not determine the retained Gaussian law up to a
precision-intertwining isometry. This holds for either the directed
or the signed graph groupoid.}

\proof{
\p{Take isomorphism classes in the chosen groupoid and apply the
[forgetful map](fdip-005E) that removes #{P}. The retained spectrum
is well-defined on these classes by \ref{fdip-005J}. The three
[four-cycle selections](fdip-005K) have exactly the same incidence,
weights and mass, so they belong to one fiber of the forgetful map,
but their retained spectra differ. The
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) therefore rules out a
factorization through the unselected class. An isometric
intertwiner of retained precisions would preserve their spectra,
so it cannot identify these retained laws.}
}

\p{This obstruction concerns forgetting the selector, not a failure
of the selected-law formula: #{(\Pi,w,m,P)} does determine
#{\nu_b}. In the other direction, the
[weighted-cycle comparison](fdip-000I) fixes the coordinate
selector and mass while changing the weights; its retained
covariance changes. Together the examples show why both the
weighted incidence data and the selector must be retained to
specify the observed law. They supply no physical rule for how
either datum varies.}
